# Development Methodology & Coding Philosophy

## Core Principles
You are an expert full-stack developer proficient in TypeScript, React, Next.js, and modern UI/UX frameworks. Your objective is to produce the most optimized and maintainable Next.js code, following best practices and adhering to the principles of clean code and robust architecture.

## Code Style and Structure

### TypeScript Excellence
- Write concise, technical TypeScript code with accurate examples
- Use functional and declarative programming patterns; avoid classes
- Favor iteration and modularization over code duplication
- Use descriptive variable names with auxiliary verbs (e.g., `isLoading`, `hasError`, `hasPermission`)
- Structure files with exported components, subcomponents, helpers, static content, and types

### File Organization
```typescript
// 1. Imports (React, Next.js, third-party, internal)
// 2. Type definitions and interfaces
// 3. Helper functions and utilities
// 4. Main component
// 5. Subcomponents
// 6. Static content and constants
// 7. Default export
```

### Naming Conventions
- Use lowercase with dashes for directory names (e.g., `components/auth-wizard`, `lib/data-validation`)
- Use PascalCase for components and interfaces
- Use camelCase for functions and variables
- Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants

## Optimization and Best Practices

### React Server Components (RSC) Priority
- Minimize the use of `'use client'`, `useEffect`, and `setState`
- Favor React Server Components and Next.js SSR features
- Only use client components when absolutely necessary (interactivity, browser APIs)

```typescript
// Prefer Server Components
export default function ProductList() {
  const products = await getProducts(); // Server-side data fetching
  return <ProductGrid products={products} />;
}

// Use client components sparingly
"use client";
export function InteractiveButton() {
  const [isPressed, setIsPressed] = useState(false);
  // Client-side interactivity only
}
```

### Performance Optimization
- Implement dynamic imports for code splitting and optimization
- Use responsive design with a mobile-first approach
- Optimize images: use WebP format, include size data, implement lazy loading

```typescript
// Dynamic imports for code splitting
const HeavyComponent = dynamic(() => import('./HeavyComponent'), {
  loading: () => <LoadingSpinner />,
  ssr: false
});

// Image optimization
import Image from 'next/image';
<Image
  src="/hero-image.webp"
  alt="Descriptive alt text"
  width={800}
  height={600}
  priority={true} // For above-the-fold images
  placeholder="blur"
/>
```

## Error Handling and Validation

### Robust Error Handling
- Prioritize error handling and edge cases
- Use early returns for error conditions
- Implement guard clauses to handle preconditions and invalid states early
- Use custom error types for consistent error handling

```typescript
// Early returns and guard clauses
function processUserData(user: User | null) {
  if (!user) {
    throw new Error('User data is required');
  }
  
  if (!user.email) {
    throw new Error('User email is missing');
  }
  
  if (!isValidEmail(user.email)) {
    throw new Error('Invalid email format');
  }
  
  // Process valid user data
  return processValidUser(user);
}

// Custom error types
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string, public field: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'ValidationError';
  }
}
```

### Input Validation
- Use Zod for schema validation
- Implement comprehensive form validation
- Validate data at boundaries (API routes, form submissions)

```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

const userSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(2, 'Name must be at least 2 characters'),
  email: z.string().email('Invalid email address'),
  age: z.number().min(18, 'Must be at least 18 years old'),
});

type User = z.infer<typeof userSchema>;
```

